# YouTube Daily Automation — Project Memory

## What this project is
A fully automated "faceless" YouTube channel pipeline built as an **n8n workflow** plus a
set of **local Python helper scripts** in `scripts/`.

**Working copy of the workflow: `YouTube-Daily-Automation.json` in the project root** — this
is the edited/corrected version we maintain and import into n8n. The original untouched
export is `C:\Users\Hunani Infotech\Downloads\YouTube Daily Automation (1).json` (do not
edit; keep as backup reference). It runs once a day, discovers a trending tech topic, writes a script with an
LLM, synthesizes a voiceover, assembles a video from stock footage, generates a thumbnail,
and uploads the result to YouTube — logging everything to Google Sheets and Airtable.

## End-to-end pipeline (n8n node order)
1. **Daily Trigger** — schedule trigger, fires at 08:00.
2. Fan-out to 3 sources (HTTP): **Hacker News** (Algolia front_page), **Dev.to** trending,
   **YouTube Trending** (`YOUTUBE_API_KEY` env).
3. **Merge HN and Trends** → **Merge All Sources** (merge nodes).
4. **Score and Pick Topic** (Code) — scores every candidate (points + comments*5, or
   views/1000), then applies a **2.5x `cpmBoost()`** to titles matching high-CPM keywords
   (ai, llm, saas, startup, funding, enterprise, security, fintech, business, etc.) so the
   channel drifts toward higher-CPM tech, and picks the single highest-scoring title.
5. **Log Topic to Sheets** — appends `date, topic, used` to a Google Sheet.
6. **Generate Script (Groq)** — POST to Groq `chat/completions`, model
   `llama-3.1-8b-instant`, `max_tokens: 4096`, asks for strict JSON:
   `title, description, sections[], script`. Prompt is the **Nexiora editorial voice**:
   an 8-10 min (~1200-1500 word) opinionated take (what it means, who wins/loses, a
   prediction) with a curiosity hook + subscribe CTA — NOT a plain summary.
7. **Parse Script JSON** (Code) — heavy defensive JSON repair (small model emits invalid
   JSON: unescaped newlines, truncation, split strings, leaked chat tokens). DO NOT
   casually simplify this — each branch fixes an observed real failure.
8. **Build Full Script** → **Convert Script To Text File** → **Save Script To Disk**.
9. **Generate Voiceover (Local TTS)** — `executeCommand` runs `scripts/tts_edge.py`
   (edge-tts) → `voice.mp3`.
10. **Read Voiceover Audio** → **Save Audio to Drive** + **Prepare Voiceover Data**.
11. **Extract Keywords** (Code) — turns section headings into short stock-search queries.
12. **Split By Keyword** (splitInBatches loop) → **Fetch Pexels Clips** → **Pick Best Clip**
    → **Assign Clip Path** → **Download Clip Binary** → **Save Clip To Disk** → back to loop.
13. **Collect All Clips** → **Prepare Render Manifest** (Code, builds `manifest.json`) →
    Convert/Save manifest → **Render Video (Local FFmpeg)** runs `scripts/render_video.py`.
14. **Read Rendered Video** → **Attach Video Metadata** → **Save Video to Drive**.
15. **Thumbnail (LOCAL)** — `Prepare Thumbnail Title` (Code, exposes `thumbTitle`) →
    `Convert Title To Text File` (convertToFile) → `Save Thumb Title To Disk` (write
    `thumb_title.txt`) → `Generate Thumbnail (Local)` (executeCommand runs
    `scripts/generate_thumbnail.py <thumb_title.txt> <thumbnail.png>`) → `Read Thumbnail File`
    (reads `thumbnail.png` into binary property **`thumbnail`**). Replaced the old remote
    Render service (`n8n-media-service.onrender.com`) + Wait node.
16. **Calculate Chapters** (Code — also appends the Nexiora subscribe CTA + `#Nexiora
    #TechNews #AI` footer to the description) → **Set Publish Time** (tomorrow **5:00 PM
    US Eastern**, DST-safe via `Intl` America/New_York) → **Upload to YouTube** (private +
    `publishAt`, **category 28 Science & Technology, regionCode US**, `tags` set to Nexiora
    keyword list) → **Save to Airtable**.
17. **Send Error Email** (Gmail) — currently an ORPHAN node (see issues).

## Local helper scripts (`scripts/`)
- `tts_edge.py` — `edge_tts` CLI wrapper: `tts_edge.py <textfile> <out.mp3> [voice]`
  (default voice `en-US-JennyNeural`).
- `tts_edge.ps1` — PowerShell wrapper around `tts_edge.py`.
- `render_video.py` — reads a manifest (`clips[], audio, title, output, clipLength`),
  builds an ffmpeg `filter_complex` that scales/crops each clip to 1280x720@30, trims to
  `clipLength` (default 8s), concats, overlays the title for the first 3s, muxes the
  voiceover with `-shortest`. Needs `ffmpeg` on PATH.
- `generate_thumbnail.py` — the ACTIVE thumbnail generator (ffmpeg + drawtext, 1280x720),
  now wired into the workflow (see node order step 15). Args: `<title_file> <output.png>
  [bg_image]`. **Varies per title** (deterministic md5 seed): rotates 6 on-brand Nexiora
  palettes + 3 text positions, adds a "NEXIORA" watermark, and accepts an optional 3rd arg
  (a video frame) to use as a darkened background — kills the "identical template"
  inauthentic-content signal. Font is env-driven (`YT_FONT_BOLD`/`YT_FONT_FILE`, falls back
  to Windows arialbd). To also use a video frame bg, extend the executeCommand with a 3rd arg
  (e.g. a frame extracted from `final_video.mp4`) — not enabled by default.

## Paths are now ENV-DRIVEN (not hardcoded)
The workflow no longer contains machine-specific paths. It reads them from env vars, so the
SAME workflow JSON runs on Windows, the Docker container, or a cloud VM:
- `YT_SCRIPTS_DIR` — dir containing the `scripts/*.py` helpers
- `YT_TMP_DIR` — working/temp dir (script.txt, voice.mp3, clip_*.mp4, manifest.json, final_video.mp4)
- `YT_FONT_FILE` / `YT_FONT_BOLD` — font paths for ffmpeg drawtext (render_video.py / generate_thumbnail.py; default to Windows arial if unset)

**PORTABLE across two Windows PCs (owner zips + moves the folder home laptop <-> office PC,
different usernames).** Do NOT hardcode a username anywhere — paths are derived at launch:
**Easiest local launch: `run-n8n.ps1`** (repo root) — sets `YT_SCRIPTS_DIR=<repo>/scripts`
(repo-relative) and `YT_TMP_DIR=<%USERPROFILE%>/.n8n-files/tmp` (per-user, resolves on any
username), sets fonts + `N8N_BLOCK_ENV_ACCESS_IN_NODE=false`, creates the tmp dir, dot-sources
`env.local.ps1` for secrets, auto-discovers ffmpeg on PATH (WinGet fallback), then runs `npx n8n`.
Run: `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File run-n8n.ps1`.
- **Secrets in tracked files:** fill `env.local.ps1` (`$env:YOUTUBE_API_KEY = "..."`)
  and `credentials-export.json`. Keep the GitHub repo private if real keys are present.
  The launcher warns if `YOUTUBE_API_KEY` is unset.
- `automation/_common.py` `TMP_DIR` also resolves via `YT_TMP_DIR` env or `Path.home()/.n8n-files/tmp`.
- CAVEAT: n8n's imported workflow + credentials live in `%USERPROFILE%\.n8n` (OUTSIDE the zip),
  so on the second PC you must re-import the workflow and re-enter credentials (or copy `.n8n`).
- **`setup-second-pc.ps1`** automates the fresh-PC setup: checks python, installs ffmpeg
  (winget) + python deps, creates the tmp dir, scaffolds `env.local.ps1`, and runs
  `npx n8n import:workflow`. It then prints the manual steps (paste `YOUTUBE_API_KEY`,
  reconnect the 7 credentials, or copy `.n8n` + share `N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY`). See `README.md`.
Code nodes read them via `$env.YT_TMP_DIR` — needs `N8N_BLOCK_ENV_ACCESS_IN_NODE=false`.

